{"attack":true,"case":"The conjecture that validity of every committed root reduces exactly to 'no enrichment adds an attacker to a committed root' is false, because deleting an attacker from a committed root's support chain is an equally validity-destroying move that the load-bearing check cannot catch.\nThe proposal restricts admissible enrichments to functions that add attack edges monotonically with no deletions to the subgraph induced by non-committed-root nodes. But monotonicity of the attack relation (att) does not imply monotonicity of a root's supporting argument chain in the induced subgraph. A committed root r can have a non-root defender d whose own status depends on the absence of attacks against d. Adding an edge x->d (x non-root, d non-root) does not attack r directly, so it passes the single load-bearing check, yet it can turn d from IN to OUT. If r depended on d, r is no longer reinstated, so r's validity is lost even though no attacker was added on r. Thus the check is insufficient and the exact-reduction claim is false.\ncites: e093eb8611ff0d96fc197357178adfbf4e9e7c0ba8471f3c76aa9f21a3aea46a","counterexample":["Nodes: r (committed root), d (non-root defender), c (committed root), x (non-root attacker).","Base edges: d->r, x->c, c->d. In the base graph r is IN (d defends it), c is IN (no attacker), d is IN (x is OUT because c->d reinstates d after x->d; with c->d, x is defeated so x->d does not defeat d).","Enrichment adds edge x->d (monotone, no deletion, no attacker on a committed root).","After enrichment the grounded fixpoint yields c OUT (x->c now unopposed), d OUT (x->d now unopposed), r OUT. r was a committed root and is now refuted, so the validity guarantee is violated while 'no new attacker on a root' held."]}